{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '420,470p' src/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc; rg -n 'dial-reflect|resolve|channel-descriptor|remote-descriptor' src/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc; sed -n '1881,1930p' src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc; rg -n 'close\"'!|close-socket|closing'\"' src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc; sed -n '700,750p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md; cat collab/1791270823433-repl-engineer-head-h3.claude-opus-5-5.stdout.log | tail -90\"","aggregated_output":"  [dial cd n]\n  (let [{:keys [channel]} @dial]\n    (or (when (nil? @channel) (establish! dial cd))\n        ((:resolve! @channel) cd n))))\n\n\n(defn dial-reflect!\n  \"Attach a further reflection of `descriptor` through a dial's already\n   established channel, sharing the one link `dial-attach!` created for\n   it: dao.stream.remote.cljc keeps one link per channel descriptor,\n   shared by every reflection through that channel, and `attacher`'s\n   returned function is exactly that per-channel sharing boundary.  A\n   dial with no established channel yet answers `:dao.stream/invalid-\n   descriptor`, since there is no attacher to share.\"\n  [dial descriptor]\n  (let [{:keys [channel]} @dial]\n    (if-some [reflect! (:reflect! @channel)]\n      (reflect! descriptor)\n      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/invalid-descriptor})))\n\n\n(defn dial-step!\n  \"Drive the dialing composition one tick, at the composition's\n   cadence: the attachment's projection first, so what the wire\n   deposited reaches the ring, then this end's own mirror step over\n   the projected reader and the socket handle, answering the other\n   direction's requests against this end's table. Returns the\n   mirror's advanced reading cursor.\"\n  [dial]\n  (let [{:keys [ring table names channel mirror-cursor]} @dial]\n    (when-some [project (:project @channel)]\n      (step! project))\n    (when-some [handle (:handle @channel)]\n      (swap! mirror-cursor\n             (fn [c] (remote/mirror-step table names ring c handle))))\n    @mirror-cursor))\n\n\n(defn channel\n  \"The dialed channel as data: {:attachment id :handle h :project p} --\n   the ws attachment identity, the ws handle (the channel writer), and\n   the attachment's projection. Nil before the first dial-attach!. A\n   composition that owns the connection's lifetime closes the handle\n   here.\"\n  [dial]\n  @(:channel @dial))\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:329:   that this end's mirror answers named requests from. `dial-resolve!`\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:330:   resolves a name through the dialed channel before any reflection\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:381:                         :resolve! (:resolve ls)})\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:410:(defn dial-resolve!\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:412:   `cd`: dao.stream.remote's link resolve (dao.stream.remote.md 2.4).\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:414:   does, so a name is resolved before any reflection exists; the\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:415:   descriptor a resolve answers is then attached through the same\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:416:   channel by `dial-reflect!`. A channel attach that fails answers the\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:417:   ws attacher's own outcome. Answers the resolve's outcome: retry\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:423:        ((:resolve! @channel) cd n))))\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:426:(defn dial-reflect!\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:28:   entry and `resolve` over the same per-channel links, so a name is\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:29:   resolved and its stream then attached through one link.\"\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:475:  \"A resolve's answer, read off the channel: taken only when it echoes\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:477:   the resolve that asked. No reflection exists for a name, so nothing\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:504:   resolve's (absorb-named!).\"\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:574:   by calling resolve again -- so resend-after re-sends it, and\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:786:;; The link's resolve (2.4)\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:805:   leaves the request unsent and the next resolve sends again. The\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:819:(defn- resolved\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:844:(defn- resolve-name\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:845:  \"The link's resolve: drain, then a filed answer for the name is\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:850:   refuses with full is retried by the next resolve; any other refusal\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:853:   can carry -- so each resolve of that name attempts the send again.\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:861:      (resolved link ans))\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:916:(defn- valid-remote-descriptor?\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:929:   :resolve g}. `f` is `attacher`'s entry. `(g channel-descriptor\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:930:   name)` is the link's resolve over the link `f` keeps for that\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:958:       (if-not (valid-remote-descriptor? descriptor)\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:978:     :resolve\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:982:         (resolve-name (link-for! cd) n)))}))\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:1005:   resolve beside it.\"\n(defn repl-state\n  \"A serializable summary of shell state.\n\n   The stream summaries read the ledger rather than asking a handle whether it\n   is closed: dao.stream has no `closed?`, and an idle medium has no last\n   operation.\n   The program media are the composition's own, observed beside the VM, so\n   the shell reports what it knows of them — the declared capacity, the gaps\n   the session's observers counted, and whether a loss has already refused\n   further evaluation.  Each `:pending` entry carries the link policy's\n   name (`:manual` or `:fn`) and the pending run's `:checks`, so a host\n   can display why a run is still waiting (yin.repl.link-policy.md\n   section 3.5).  `:macros` maps each macro the expander's store holds\n   to its lambda's root address (yin.vm.macro.md §10.1).  `:index` is the\n   code indexer's `yin.repl.index/status`: whether every committed program\n   is published, whether a gap lost the indexer, and its last failure.\n   `:ast-index` is the AST indexer's `yin.repl.ast-index/status`: the sizes\n   of its `$ast` and `$occ` relations, whether a gap lost it, and why it\n   failed.\"\n  [state]\n  {:lang (:lang state)\n   :macros (into (sorted-map-by #(compare (str %1) (str %2)))\n                 (map (fn [[sym entry]]\n                        [sym (first (:yin.macro/tree entry))]))\n                 (get-in state [:expander :ctx :store]))\n   :vm {:type (:vm-type state)\n        :halted? (vm/halted? (:vm state))\n        :blocked? (vm/blocked? (:vm state))\n        :in-stream {:capacity ingress-capacity\n                    :gaps (ingress-gaps state)\n                    :lost? (boolean (:ingress-loss? state))}}\n   :running? (:running? state)\n   :output {:cursor (:output-cursor state)\n            :last-outcome (get-in state [:ledger :output] :untried)}\n   :pending (when-let [parked (:pending-run state)]\n              (let [policy (:link-policy state)]\n                (mapv (fn [p]\n                        {:name (:name p)\n                         :link-id (:yin.link/id p)\n                         :policy (if (fn? policy) :fn policy)\n                         :checks (or (:checks parked) 0)})\n                      (:links parked))))\n   :index (index/status (:indexer state))\n   :ast-index (ast-index/status (:ast-indexer state))\n   :telemetry {:supported? false :note telemetry-text}\n   :remote {:connected? false}})\n6:   `:close!` functions below.  The public DaoStream surface is consequently\n117:(defn- invoke-close!\n119:  (when-let [f (:close! socket)]\n157:        (invoke-close! socket 1000 \"dao.stream/deposit-failed\")))\n218:  (close!\n226:      (when (= :failed (invoke-close! @socket 1000 \"dao.stream/detached\"))\n234:   Generic `stream/close!` is a reattachable detachment.  This helper sends the\n247:    (when (= :failed (invoke-close! @socket ended-close-code \"dao.stream/ended\"))\n263:  (invoke-close! (:socket @state) protocol-close-code \"dao.stream/protocol-error\"))\n390:   containing `:send!` and `:close!`.  It must not wait for the peer.\n408:              (if (and (map? socket) (fn? (:send! socket)) (fn? (:close! socket)))\n509:       (do (invoke-close! socket protocol-close-code \"dao.stream/subprotocol-unsupported\")\n526:           (do (invoke-close! socket 1013 \"dao.stream/acceptance-full\")\n547:                   (invoke-close! socket 1011 \"dao.stream/offer-failed\")\n624:                    (invoke-close! (:socket @hstate) protocol-close-code \"dao.stream/invalid-ack\")\n636:        (invoke-close! (:socket @hstate) 1000 \"dao.stream/acceptance-expired\")\na reader handle is obtained and who may connect to the board. It changes\nno trace, no rule, no file and no reader semantics.\n\n### 8.1 What is transport-agnostic, and stays so\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Part</th><th>Why it cannot depend on a transport</th></tr>\n<tr><td>The trace and its signature (5.2)</td>\n<td>No address, no time, no channel datum is signed.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>judge</code> (5.5)</td>\n<td>No source argument. One source is the degenerate case.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Confirmation (5.2, 5.5)</td>\n<td>The index is content-addressed and fetched by the DHT, which\nverifies every blob against its address.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Persistence (5.7)</td>\n<td><code>heads.edn</code> holds signed traces and no source.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The follower (5.3, 5.5)</td>\n<td>It holds a reader handle and calls <code>cursor</code> and\n<code>next</code>. A lost source is one event, whatever lost it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td>Reader semantics (5.6, 5.7)</td>\n<td>They start at the snapshot set.</td></tr>\n</table>\n\nOnly `yin.vm.linker.head.ws` and the shell's flags know a transport.\nSlice H1 proves the claim by test: the follower is driven to completion\nover a plain local ring, with no channel at all.\n\n### 8.2 What each later step adds and amends\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Step</th><th>Adds</th><th>Amends</th>\n<th>Changes the core's trace, rule or persistence?</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Serving off loopback</strong> (8.3)</td>\n<td>A bind that is not loopback; a bound on concurrent sessions; a\nbanner line.</td>\n<td>This document 5.1 and 5.8; <code>yin.vm.linker.head.ws</code>;\n<code>yin.repl.main</code>. Nothing in <code>dao.stream.*</code> if the\nunverified items of 8.3 hold.</td>\n<td><strong>No.</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Relaying</strong></td>\n<td>A second ring per followed principal holding the installed trace\nverbatim, read from <code>heads.edn</code>; a flag.</td>\n<td>Nothing in <code>dao.stream.*</code>: name resolution is in the\ncore (5.1), and a relay is one more peer mapping the same name to its\nown ring. This document 5.1 and 5.5; the shell.</td>\n<td><strong>No.</strong> It is why the trace carries no address and\nwhy <code>heads.edn</code> keeps the whole signed trace.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Many sources per principal</strong></td>\n<td>A cursor per source; a scheduling policy for which candidate loads\nwhen sources disagree.</td>\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-06 07:52:20 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-06 14:52:20 +07\nCoding-Agent: claude\nSession-ID: 8b4bda87-5e50-47a0-b0c9-391dba6d3ca7\n\nSlice H3 is implemented and every criterion has a passing JVM test. That includes the end-to-end process test across real loopback sockets, with both a JVM reader and a Node reader. Per the brief, the full Node and Dart lanes were not run; the orchestrator's `bb test` at landing still has to confirm them. I ran no git commands.\n\n## Changed files\n- **Source:** `src/cljc/yin/repl/dht.cljc`, `main.cljc`, `state.cljc`, `query.cljc`, `link.cljc`, `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc`.\n- **Tests:**\n  - **New:** `test/yin/repl/dht_head_test.cljc`. It is a test for `dht.cljc`; I added a file because `dht_test.cljc` is already 2,594 lines.\n  - **Changed:** `test/yin/repl/main_test.cljc`, `state_test.cljc`, `dht_process_test.clj`.\n- **Docs:** `src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/yin.repl.md`, plus the `--help` lines in `main.cljc`.\n- **Not edited:** no other file. The head design doc and `dao.stream.md` are untouched.\n\n## Commands and outcomes\n- `clj -M:kondo --lint` on all ten changed source and test files: 0 errors, 0 warnings.\n- `bb build:yin-repl-node`: built, 0 warnings.\n  - `npm ci` was refused for lack of approval. The Node leg still passed, so `ws` most likely resolved from the parent checkout's `node_modules`.\n- Final combined JVM run of 13 namespaces (the 10 `yin.repl.*` namespaces below plus `yin.vm.linker.head-test`, `head-follow-test` and `head-ws-test`): **259 tests, 2,736 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors.**\n  - `yin.repl.dht-head-test`, `main-test`, `state-test`, `dht-test`, `query-test`, `require-test`, `store-test`, `serve-test`, `connect-test`, `driver-test`.\n- Earlier focused runs:\n  - `dht-head-test`: 10 tests, 97 assertions.\n  - `main-test`: 35 tests, 276 assertions.\n  - `state-test`: 14 tests, 100 assertions.\n- `clj -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test` (all three slow tests): **3 tests, 124 assertions, 0 failures.**\n  - The new test alone: 33 assertions.\n  - Its first run had one failure, in my own wait helper: it required a publication acknowledgement that a single anchor can't give. I fixed the helper; nothing else in that run failed.\n\n## H3 criteria — all done\n1. **Token prints once, only when the board is bound; a third part is refused with the pin message.** Pinned by `the-token-prints-once-when-the-board-is-bound`, `no-board-means-no-token` (port in use, non-loopback bind, no key) and `a-token-with-a-third-part-is-refused-with-the-pin-message`. In the process test, A prints exactly one token.\n2. **`dht join` passes no `--dht-manifest` and saves `--dht-follow`; a bare restart resolves from `heads.edn` before any connection.** Pinned by `dht-join-expands-a-token-to-peer-principal-and-follow` and `dht-join-saves-its-follow-and-a-bare-start-resumes-it`. The restart part is checked at open, before any step and with no WebSocket host, in `a-reader-follows-requires-moves-and-restarts-from-heads-edn`.\n3. **An undeclared followed principal refuses startup; `--dht-manifest` suspends following for that run.** Pinned by `dht-follow-of-an-undeclared-principal-refuses-startup` and `dht-manifest-suspends-following-for-the-run`.\n4. **A require typed before the first head parks and completes on the install; `(abandon)` ends it.** Pinned by `a-reader-follows-…` and `abandon-ends-a-first-contact-require`. The process test also checks that the first require prints a pending line.\n5. **The write precedes the install.** Pinned by `the-write-precedes-the-install`: a failing write prints `:yin.head/unpersisted (disk full)`, installs nothing, and installs after the retry.\n   - Both kill cases (before the write, and between write and install) are **simulated**: a write seam fails, then the shell is closed and reopened from the same directory. No real OS process is killed.\n6. **The moved line, an unchanged repeat require, and `(reset)` then require.** Pinned in-process by `a-reader-follows-…` and in the process test's `moved-leg` on both the JVM and Node readers.\n7. **`heads.edn` cases (absent, malformed, a record that fails verification, a record for an unfollowed principal).** Pinned by `heads-edn-cases-are-refusals-as-data` and `heads-edn-renders-with-no-space-before-a-closer`. These are portable tests, so they will run on Dart at landing; they have not run there yet.\n8. **End to end on JVM and Node.** Pinned by `a-reader-follows-the-publisher-s-head-across-processes`: requires on all four VMs, republish, `(reset)`, and a restart with A stopped.\n9. **The host module holds no rule.** Pinned by a JVM-only source scan, `the-host-module-holds-no-fold-verification-or-file-write`, and by `the-host-module-answers-with-no-follower`.\n\n## Shapes I chose\n- **Token:** `yin:<host:port>/<64 hex>`. The parser also accepts an `ed25519:` prefix on the principal.\n- **`--dht-follow`:** `<64 hex>@<host:port>`.\n  - The host must be a loopback literal; `localhost` becomes 127.0.0.1.\n  - It needs a `--dht-peer`, takes each principal once, and allows at most 64.\n  - In the spec it becomes `:follow [{:principal hex :host h :port p}]`. With a pin, the spec gets `:follow-suspended true` and no `:follow`.\n- **`heads.edn`:** written by `pr-str` as `{:version 1, :heads {\"ed25519:…\" trace}}`, with closed keys.\n  - It lives in the `dht:<dir>` store directory, not the node directory.\n  - For `dht` subcommands those are the same directory. **Design 5.7 says \"`<node dir>`\" and should say the store directory.**\n- **Where the follower state lives:** it is carried on the DHT node value under `:yin.repl.dht/…` keys. `query.cljc` reads it through `repl.dht/follower`.\n- **New public helpers:** `repl.dht/read-heads`, `parse-heads`, `render-heads` and `follow-lines`. `open` gains an options map: `:key`, `:ws`, `:write-heads!`.\n- **Real host binding:** only `main/startup` supplies the real WebSocket host. `boot` uses whatever `:ws-host` it is given, so in-process tests never bind real listeners.\n\n## H2 sign-off notes\n1. **Liveness is the host's.** A dial still resolving after the repair delay is closed and recomposed, as is a lost one, with the delay doubling from `:repair-ticks` to `:repair-max-ticks`. Pinned by `a-lost-or-silent-dial-is-closed-and-composed-again`.\n2. **Only the given address is dialed.** Followed addresses are loopback-only at parse and again in `open`; the tests assert the dialed address set.\n3. **Close before the next dial.** The old dial is closed both on the follower's `:source-lost` and on a lost dial, before the next is composed (asserted in the same test).\n4. **Does a failed bind hold resources?** Checked by reading host code only; I did not test leaks.\n   - **JVM:** `http-kit/run-server` throws synchronously, so `serve` refuses and keeps no listener. Whether http-kit closes its internal selector on a failed start is unverified.\n   - **Node:** `ws` emits `error` from an http server that never listened, which holds no socket.\n   - **Dart:** the `HttpServer.bind` future fails, so no server is stored.\n5. **A real listener.** The end-to-end test crosses real http-kit and Node `ws` sockets.\n\n## Owner-question defaults, and where each is baked in\n1. **No relink in a live session:** the moved line plus `(reset)`. Small change (`moved-lines` in `dht.cljc`).\n2. **Bare `q` answers the reader's own index:** this falls out of join no longer hydrating; there is no code to change.\n3. **Every HEAD move deposits:** baked into the `:head-fn` wrapper. Depositing only on `yin.link/publish` would need a signal from the indexer that a round published names: a moderate change.\n4. **WebSocket, loopback only:** enforced in three places (`parse-follow`, `checked-follow`, and the H2 `serve`). Each is a small change.\n5. **Safety without a progress guarantee:** inherited unchanged from H1's follower.\n\n## Unresolved and incomplete\n- **Node and Dart lanes not run** (per the brief). Unknown Dart risks: `pr-str` output of `heads.edn`, and the new portable test file.\n- **Kill cases are simulated,** not real process kills.\n- **A lost request on an attached reflection is not detected.** The follower would see retryable errors indefinitely. Over TCP that should surface as a closed channel and `:source-lost`, but it isn't separately handled or tested.\n- **A refused deposit is not tested.** It is reported through a 64-entry note ring and printed as a line, but no test makes a deposit refuse.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
